1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,570
Welcome to our Parents and Carers
workshop, supporting your Autistic

2
00:00:08,570 --> 00:00:09,640
child to flourish.

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:11,900
I'm glad you've made some time for this.

4
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:14,760
You might be arriving with a clear
question, or after a very full

5
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:15,380
morning.

6
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,740
Either way, you're welcome here. Today

7
00:00:17,740 --> 00:00:20,520
we'll build on what you already know about
your child,

8
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:24,860
explore some shared language, and consider
supports that fit your family.

9
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:26,600
Keep your workbook nearby.

10
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,600
In this walkthrough, activity pauses are
brief.

11
00:00:30,600 --> 00:00:33,860
Whenever you need more time to think,
write or talk,

12
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:36,500
pause the video and resume when you're
ready.

13
00:00:38,841 --> 00:00:43,763
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

14
00:00:43,764 --> 00:00:48,878
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

15
00:00:48,879 --> 00:00:53,418
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

16
00:00:53,419 --> 00:00:58,213
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

17
00:00:58,214 --> 00:01:01,409
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

18
00:01:04,440 --> 00:01:07,500
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

19
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,640
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

20
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:14,600
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

21
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:18,040
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

22
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:22,240
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

23
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:26,250
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

24
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:31,360
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

25
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:35,990
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

26
00:01:35,990 --> 00:01:37,860
Australia’s colonial history

27
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:41,570
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

28
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:46,000
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

29
00:01:49,401 --> 00:01:53,793
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

30
00:01:53,794 --> 00:01:56,319
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

31
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,600
Everyone is welcome to be themselves here.

32
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,920
You can move, stand, fidget, listen,
write,

33
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:05,400
step out or pass on an activity.

34
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:07,120
Use whatever helps you take part.

35
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:10,560
Some parents and carers here may be
Autistic

36
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,760
or otherwise neurodivergent themselves.

37
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:15,240
Your experience is welcome too.

38
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:16,680
Your workbook belongs to you,

39
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:18,600
and you decide what to share.

40
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,120
Please keep other families' stories
private.

41
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,680
When we discuss school or family
situations,

42
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:26,200
leave out identifying details.

43
00:02:26,200 --> 00:02:29,280
We can learn from a situation while
respecting the people involved.

44
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,430
Before we settle in, take a moment to
check your surroundings: the

45
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:38,640
exits, bathroom facilities, refreshments
and any quiet space available.

46
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:43,240
Follow the arrangements for your venue,
including smoking and vaping.

47
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:47,560
If you're watching elsewhere, make the
space as comfortable as you can.

48
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:51,440
Keep your phone available if you need to
be contacted by school or a

49
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,600
carer, and be considerate about sounds and
calls.

50
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:57,520
Have your workbook and something to write
with nearby.

51
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:01,420
You can write questions down, and in a
live workshop you can ask the

52
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:03,560
delivery team privately or during a break.

53
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,740
Attend to your own needs as we go.

54
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:11,900
Let's meet the people around us. At your
table, share your first name

55
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:13,600
and who you're here for.

56
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:18,240
You might include your child's age or year
level, if you'd like to.

57
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:22,000
Then share one thing you would like to
understand by the end of the day.

58
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,040
Keep it brief so everyone has room, and
remember you can pass.

59
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:32,470
There's no need to share a diagnosis,
school name or private family

60
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:33,280
circumstances.

61
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,880
If you're watching on your own, make a
note of your question on page four

62
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:37,920
of the workbook.

63
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,520
Pause here for your introductions.

64
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:51,520
Let's come back. Write your one thing on
page four if you haven't

65
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:52,040
already.

66
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:56,600
Keep that question with you. We'll return
to it in our final session

67
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:00,830
and consider what has become clearer, as
well as what you'd still like

68
00:04:00,830 --> 00:04:01,480
to explore.

69
00:04:03,880 --> 00:04:05,880
Here's the shape of our day.

70
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:09,960
We begin with your child as a whole
person, then explore sensory

71
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:11,880
experience and thinking.

72
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,080
After morning tea, we move into
communication, connection and daily

73
00:04:16,080 --> 00:04:16,880
capacity.

74
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:21,720
The afternoon turns towards home and
community, followed by partnership

75
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:22,880
with school.

76
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:27,880
Our final session brings the learning back
to a few small next steps.

77
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:32,890
The timetable on the slide is a guide for
a live workshop, including

78
00:04:32,890 --> 00:04:38,880
morning tea from eleven to eleven fifteen
and lunch from twelve fifteen to twelve
forty-five.

79
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,880
In this recording, take breaks when you
need them.

80
00:04:42,880 --> 00:04:46,830
These six sessions are connected, so keep
the same child in mind

81
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:47,880
throughout.

82
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:52,240
A quick word about language.

83
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:57,240
You'll hear us say Autistic child or
Autistic person.

84
00:04:57,240 --> 00:05:02,480
Many Autistic people prefer this
identity-first language because autism

85
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:04,040
is part of who they are.

86
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:08,400
Some prefer person on the autism spectrum,
or another description.

87
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,100
Each person's preference matters.

88
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:15,520
Follow your child's lead wherever
possible, and be open to their

89
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,280
preferences changing.

90
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:21,800
You may still be finding words that feel
comfortable in your family.

91
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:24,620
There's no test on terminology today.

92
00:05:24,620 --> 00:05:28,040
Our purpose is to speak respectfully and
to listen to how people

93
00:05:28,040 --> 00:05:29,480
describe themselves.

94
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:34,040
Bring your child to mind.

95
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:38,140
What are three things you love or value
about them?

96
00:05:38,140 --> 00:05:39,840
It might be their humour,

97
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:42,040
an interest they know so much about,

98
00:05:42,040 --> 00:05:43,840
the way they connect with someone,

99
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:46,640
or a small moment of joy.

100
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:50,240
Write those things on page four.

101
00:05:50,240 --> 00:05:52,740
For this moment, put the list of
challenges to one side

102
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:54,640
and notice who they are.

103
00:05:54,640 --> 00:05:56,040
If this is a hard season,

104
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,840
one small moment from the past week is
enough.

105
00:05:58,840 --> 00:06:00,240
Start privately,

106
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:04,040
then share one thing with your table if
you wish.

107
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:08,040
Allow four minutes for this reflection and
any sharing.

108
00:06:08,040 --> 00:06:12,040
Pause the video now, then resume when you
are ready to come back.

109
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,680
Keep that page handy.

110
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:25,840
Those strengths and joys belong in every
conversation we have today,

111
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:28,200
including conversations about support.

112
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:29,880
We'll come back to them at the end,

113
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,280
when you choose a strength to name to your
child.

114
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,640
You know your child in ways that nobody
else does.

115
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:40,720
You know the familiar routines, the tiny
changes, and what a good

116
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:42,560
moment looks like in your family.

117
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,580
That knowledge matters here.

118
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:50,040
We bring information about
neurodiversity-affirming practice to put

119
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:52,040
alongside your experience.

120
00:06:52,040 --> 00:06:55,580
As we talk it through, you can ask what
fits, what doesn't, and what

121
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:57,340
you still want to understand.

122
00:06:57,340 --> 00:07:01,600
You don't have to leave with someone
else's version of family life.

123
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:05,940
We are looking for ideas you can consider
with your own child.

124
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:11,000
We're going to hear directly from Autistic
young people.

125
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,480
Listen to their accounts on their own
terms.

126
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:16,520
Each speaker offers their own experience.

127
00:07:16,520 --> 00:07:18,400
We don't need to compare them with your
child

128
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:21,720
or ask one person to represent everyone.

129
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:22,920
Notice what stays with you,

130
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:26,160
and what it makes you curious to
understand more deeply.

131
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:27,000
Let's listen.

132
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:54,680
Take a quiet moment with what you've just
heard.

133
00:09:58,240 --> 00:10:01,520
On page four, return to the young people's
voices.

134
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,920
What did you notice?

135
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:06,260
What stayed with you? And what would you
like to understand more deeply

136
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:07,800
about your own child?

137
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:10,000
Begin with a little private writing.

138
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,240
If you share, stay close to what the
speakers actually said.

139
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:17,230
It's fine to feel moved, uncertain, or to
have a question without an

140
00:10:17,230 --> 00:10:17,920
answer.

141
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:21,800
You can talk with someone nearby or keep
this reflection to yourself.

142
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:23,440
Pause the video now.

143
00:10:31,760 --> 00:10:34,640
As we come back, keep one question in
mind.

144
00:10:34,640 --> 00:10:37,600
Sensory experience, thinking and
communication

145
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:39,600
will give us ways to explore it.

146
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:42,160
A question that stays open can be useful.

147
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:44,480
We can listen carefully without assuming

148
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:47,680
we already know what an experience means
for someone else.

149
00:10:49,960 --> 00:10:54,680
Neurodiversity means the diversity of
human brains and minds.

150
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:56,240
It includes everyone.

151
00:10:56,240 --> 00:10:59,080
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
patterns

152
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:00,680
within that wider picture,

153
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:03,320
and autism is one expression of it.

154
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:06,240
These words help us talk about differences

155
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:08,320
in experience and processing.

156
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:11,000
They don't tell us everything about a
particular person.

157
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,760
As we explore common patterns today,

158
00:11:13,760 --> 00:11:16,880
keep asking how they do, or don't,

159
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:19,200
connect with the child you know.

160
00:11:19,200 --> 00:11:21,200
Each child remains an individual.

161
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:26,750
A neurodiversity-affirming approach
recognises different ways of

162
00:11:26,750 --> 00:11:28,800
knowing, doing, being and

163
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:30,240
belonging.

164
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:33,360
People think and learn in different ways.

165
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:37,400
Everyone has strengths, and everyone can
experience challenges.

166
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:40,000
Take the things you wrote about your child
earlier.

167
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,060
Their interests, enjoyment and way of
connecting are part of the picture

168
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:43,960
when we think about

169
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:45,440
support.

170
00:11:45,440 --> 00:11:49,640
We can take difficulties seriously while
valuing who they are.

171
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:52,830
That means asking what the surroundings,
routines and support need to

172
00:11:52,830 --> 00:11:53,920
look like so your child

173
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:56,840
can participate comfortably and
authentically.

174
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:00,020
A hard day doesn't cancel out a strength,
and recognising a strength

175
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:00,680
doesn't make a

176
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:01,880
hard day disappear.

177
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:07,480
Autism is one part of a person's identity.

178
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:10,560
Culture, language, gender, age,
disability, faith

179
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:14,320
and family circumstances can overlap and
shape opportunities,

180
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:15,660
barriers and experiences.

181
00:12:15,660 --> 00:12:18,400
That's the idea of intersectionality.

182
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:23,480
The words on this slide are prompts to
widen our understanding.

183
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:26,960
You don't need to share personal identity
information here.

184
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:28,960
For your child, useful support takes
account

185
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:32,040
of the whole person and the family
context.

186
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:35,490
We can stay curious about parts of their
experience we haven't yet

187
00:12:35,490 --> 00:12:36,040
understood.

188
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:42,000
Capacity can change, sometimes within the
same day.

189
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,280
Something your child managed on a quiet
Saturday

190
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:48,240
might be much harder after a full Monday
at school.

191
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:51,920
The child is the same, but the demands,
sensory load

192
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:54,840
and available energy may be different.

193
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:57,560
The phrase dynamic disability helps us

194
00:12:57,560 --> 00:12:59,800
keep that changing experience in view.

195
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:02,480
When something becomes difficult,

196
00:13:02,480 --> 00:13:07,280
ask what else is going on and what support
fits now.

197
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,040
Yesterday's capacity doesn't tell us

198
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:12,440
exactly what is available today.

199
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:13,960
We'll come back to this in more detail

200
00:13:13,960 --> 00:13:15,600
when we talk about daily energy.

201
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:20,440
Before we finish this first session, let's
look at your rights at

202
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:20,840
school.

203
00:13:20,840 --> 00:13:25,640
The Disability Discrimination Act of
nineteen ninety-two makes it unlawful to
treat

204
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:29,300
someone unfairly because of disability,
including in education.

205
00:13:29,300 --> 00:13:34,220
The Disability Standards for Education
were introduced in two thousand and five.

206
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:38,500
These standards set out schools'
responsibilities for access and

207
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:39,760
participation.

208
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:44,460
The school must consult your child, or you
on their behalf, about

209
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:47,900
reasonable adjustments so they can take
part on the same basis as

210
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:49,920
students without disability.

211
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:55,120
That consultation can include your child
directly, you as their parent

212
00:13:55,120 --> 00:13:56,560
or carer, or both.

213
00:13:56,560 --> 00:13:59,380
Here are three useful questions to keep:

214
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:01,080
How will we be consulted?

215
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:03,080
What adjustments are in place?

216
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:05,180
How and when will they be reviewed?

217
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:10,340
This is general information, rather than
advice about an individual

218
00:14:10,340 --> 00:14:11,540
legal situation.

219
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:16,260
The Department of Education has
plain-language guides for families.

220
00:14:16,260 --> 00:14:21,310
We'll return to consultation, support
pathways and preparing for a

221
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:23,500
conversation with school in Session Five.

222
00:14:23,500 --> 00:14:27,180
Knowing your rights gives you a useful
starting point for that

223
00:14:27,180 --> 00:14:29,340
partnership.

224
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:33,440
Let's hold on to joy and flourishing as we
move forward.

225
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:37,240
Connection, belonging and small moments of
enjoyment matter.

226
00:14:37,240 --> 00:14:39,000
Every family is different, and
understanding

227
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,920
what helps can take time.

228
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:43,400
Doing something differently is fine

229
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:44,640
when it helps your family.

230
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:48,240
A quieter outing, a familiar shared
activity

231
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:50,680
or a small success can matter just as much

232
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,140
as a bigger milestone.

233
00:14:52,140 --> 00:14:55,760
In our next session, we'll begin with

234
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:57,720
how the world arrives through the senses,

235
00:14:57,720 --> 00:15:00,240
and then explore attention and thinking.

236
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:02,960
Keep your child's strengths alongside your
questions.

237
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:11,920
Welcome to Session Two.

238
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:15,000
Keep in mind the child you wrote about
earlier.

239
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,480
We're going to build a fuller picture of
their experience,

240
00:15:17,480 --> 00:15:20,480
beginning with sensory processing, then
thinking

241
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:22,480
and internal signals.

242
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:26,200
Each idea is something to explore
alongside what you know.

243
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:29,400
You may recognise some patterns and not
others.

244
00:15:29,400 --> 00:15:30,920
By the end, you'll have some questions

245
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:33,440
to take into everyday routines at home.

246
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:38,760
Before thinking, communication or
connection, there is sensory

247
00:15:38,760 --> 00:15:39,960
experience.

248
00:15:39,960 --> 00:15:42,960
The world is already arriving through our
senses.

249
00:15:42,960 --> 00:15:45,960
How it arrives can affect how comfortable
we feel,

250
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:49,960
what we notice and how much energy is
available for everything else.

251
00:15:49,960 --> 00:15:50,960
Let's start there.

252
00:15:52,680 --> 00:15:55,400
What does sensory processing mean to you?

253
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:57,120
Take a moment to put it into your own
words.

254
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:58,920
You might think about an ordinary
experience,

255
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:01,240
such as walking into a noisy room

256
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:02,760
or putting on a warm jumper.

257
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:06,160
Pause here if you'd like time to talk it
through.

258
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:19,730
Sensory processing is the way the brain
receives, processes and responds

259
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:20,480
to information from

260
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:22,140
the senses.

261
00:16:22,140 --> 00:16:25,670
We often notice the response first, but
there is a whole process

262
00:16:25,670 --> 00:16:27,480
happening before that response

263
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:28,440
becomes visible.

264
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:35,140
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell.

265
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:37,680
Here we include three more.

266
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:42,160
Proprioception is our sense of the body in
space, drawing on

267
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:44,580
information from muscles and joints.

268
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:47,840
It helps us know where our body is and how
much force we're using.

269
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:52,160
The vestibular sense relates to balance
and movement.

270
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:56,890
Interoception is our sense of internal
body signals, such as hunger,

271
00:16:56,890 --> 00:16:59,200
thirst or needing the toilet.

272
00:17:00,180 --> 00:17:01,160
We'll return to that later.

273
00:17:01,160 --> 00:17:03,420
These senses work together.

274
00:17:03,420 --> 00:17:07,290
In a busy routine, someone may be managing
several kinds of sensory

275
00:17:07,290 --> 00:17:08,580
information at once,

276
00:17:08,580 --> 00:17:11,280
including input that others barely notice.

277
00:17:13,920 --> 00:17:16,920
Think about taking off a jumper because
you're getting hot.

278
00:17:16,920 --> 00:17:19,920
First, information arrives from the body.

279
00:17:19,920 --> 00:17:24,300
The brain processes it, including what
similar experiences have felt

280
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:24,920
like before.

281
00:17:24,920 --> 00:17:28,920
Then there is a response, such as taking
the jumper off.

282
00:17:28,920 --> 00:17:31,920
This process helps us understand our
surroundings,

283
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:34,920
keep track of our body and engage with
what's happening.

284
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:38,920
People process sensory information
differently.

285
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:41,920
A visible reaction is just one part of
that process.

286
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:46,410
Before judging the reaction, it helps to
ask what information may have

287
00:17:46,410 --> 00:17:46,920
arrived,

288
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:48,920
and how it might have felt.

289
00:17:51,040 --> 00:17:53,320
We're going to watch two short clips.

290
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:56,280
The first gives a sense of sensory
overwhelm.

291
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:59,480
The second makes space for sensory joy.

292
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:03,200
Both belong in our understanding of
Autistic experience.

293
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:05,160
If the sound or images feel too much,

294
00:18:05,160 --> 00:18:08,880
you're welcome to lower the volume, look
away or pause.

295
00:18:08,880 --> 00:18:10,720
Listen to the account without assuming

296
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:14,240
every Autistic person experiences things
in the same way.

297
00:20:40,960 --> 00:20:42,960
Let's leave a little space before we move
on.

298
00:20:46,520 --> 00:20:50,360
Now let's hear about sensory joy. Notice
the pleasure and richness that

299
00:20:50,360 --> 00:20:52,120
sensory experience can bring,

300
00:20:52,120 --> 00:20:55,120
alongside the overwhelm we've just
considered.

301
00:21:47,400 --> 00:21:50,600
Sensory experience includes comfort and
enjoyment too.

302
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:54,360
A sound, movement or texture can be deeply
satisfying.

303
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:57,440
Keep both sides in the picture when you
think about your child:

304
00:21:57,440 --> 00:22:00,990
what asks a lot of their senses, and what
feels good or helps them

305
00:22:00,990 --> 00:22:01,440
settle.

306
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:07,720
This framework from Winnie Dunn describes
four patterns of sensory

307
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:08,480
response.

308
00:22:08,480 --> 00:22:12,840
Registration means some input may not be
picked up readily.

309
00:22:12,840 --> 00:22:18,200
Sensory seeking means actively looking for
more input, perhaps through

310
00:22:18,200 --> 00:22:19,800
movement, touch or sound.

311
00:22:19,800 --> 00:22:25,470
Sensory sensitivity means noticing input
strongly, even if the person

312
00:22:25,470 --> 00:22:26,800
doesn't act to change it.

313
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:31,950
Sensory avoiding means actively reducing
input, such as covering ears or

314
00:22:31,950 --> 00:22:33,800
choosing a quieter place.

315
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:37,920
A person may seek one kind of input and
avoid another.

316
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:42,550
The pattern can also shift with the
setting and the load already being

317
00:22:42,550 --> 00:22:43,400
carried.

318
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:47,640
Some families will have encountered a
sensory profile through an

319
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:49,080
occupational therapist.

320
00:22:49,080 --> 00:22:53,360
Use this as a way to notice patterns and
ask questions.

321
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:56,480
It doesn't give us a complete description
of a child.

322
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:02,120
Stimming means repetitive movement or
sound,

323
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:06,040
such as rocking, flapping, spinning,
humming or tapping.

324
00:23:06,040 --> 00:23:10,320
It can help a child settle, focus, cope or
express joy.

325
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:12,440
You may recognise small versions in
yourself,

326
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:14,040
such as jiggling your leg.

327
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:18,320
Notice what stimming seems to do for your
child.

328
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:19,520
Make room for it at home,

329
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:22,120
and help others understand its purpose.

330
00:23:22,120 --> 00:23:23,240
If something causes harm,

331
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:26,240
the question becomes how to meet the same
need more safely.

332
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:27,720
Simply taking it away

333
00:23:27,720 --> 00:23:29,720
can remove something that was helping.

334
00:23:29,720 --> 00:23:32,800
Some children hold their stims back at
school.

335
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:34,880
Home can offer room for authentic
expression

336
00:23:34,880 --> 00:23:36,280
without that extra effort.

337
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:41,840
Turn to the sensory activity on page six.

338
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:44,120
Choose one familiar home routine:

339
00:23:44,120 --> 00:23:47,240
getting ready for school, a meal or bath
time.

340
00:23:47,240 --> 00:23:49,680
What sensory demands are involved?

341
00:23:49,680 --> 00:23:52,200
Think about sound, light, touch, movement

342
00:23:52,200 --> 00:23:55,040
and the less visible body signals too.

343
00:23:55,040 --> 00:23:58,400
What small change could make it easier

344
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:00,400
or offer more choice?

345
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:03,400
And what would you like to find out from
your child?

346
00:24:03,400 --> 00:24:07,040
Write the routine, the demand and a
possible support.

347
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:11,000
Talk through your ideas with a partner or
table if you wish.

348
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,400
Pause here for the activity.

349
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:24,400
Look back at your notes. Keep what you
observed separate from what you

350
00:24:24,400 --> 00:24:26,000
think it might mean.

351
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:30,560
A loud bathroom or an uncomfortable
texture gives us a useful question

352
00:24:30,560 --> 00:24:31,640
to explore.

353
00:24:31,640 --> 00:24:34,900
Your child's own communication will help
us understand whether the

354
00:24:34,900 --> 00:24:37,040
change actually helps.

355
00:24:37,040 --> 00:24:39,560
Start with one workable adjustment to the
routine.

356
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:45,610
Now we move from how the world arrives to
how the mind takes it in and

357
00:24:45,610 --> 00:24:48,400
where attention goes.

358
00:24:48,400 --> 00:24:52,880
The next ideas describe patterns some
Autistic people recognise.

359
00:24:52,880 --> 00:24:56,050
Keep checking them against the person you
know, rather than assuming

360
00:24:56,050 --> 00:24:57,280
every pattern applies.

361
00:24:59,480 --> 00:25:03,800
The detail-first idea describes building
understanding from individual

362
00:25:03,800 --> 00:25:05,960
pieces towards a whole picture.

363
00:25:05,960 --> 00:25:10,660
Someone may notice a small change, an
inconsistency or a background

364
00:25:10,660 --> 00:25:13,720
detail before the overall situation
becomes clear.

365
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:17,720
This can be connected with strengths such
as pattern recognition, deep

366
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:22,320
memory, logical precision, honesty and
close attention.

367
00:25:22,320 --> 00:25:26,720
Think about what your child knows in
detail, or what they notice that

368
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:28,200
other people miss.

369
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:31,120
These strengths are present in everyday
life.

370
00:25:31,120 --> 00:25:34,160
Taking in a lot of detail can also take
effort.

371
00:25:34,160 --> 00:25:38,650
A task may be hard to begin if the larger
picture or first step is

372
00:25:38,650 --> 00:25:39,600
unclear.

373
00:25:39,600 --> 00:25:43,920
The same way of processing can bring depth
and also create demands.

374
00:25:43,920 --> 00:25:48,350
We can notice both without turning this
into a checklist that every

375
00:25:48,350 --> 00:25:49,600
child must fit.

376
00:25:51,440 --> 00:25:53,080
Think about your own attention for a
moment.

377
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:54,920
Does it move between several things,

378
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:57,760
or become deeply absorbed in one?

379
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:00,160
The torch and floodlight image gives us

380
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:02,000
two ways to picture that.

381
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:05,240
Monotropism describes attention going
deeply

382
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:07,320
into fewer things at a time.

383
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:09,240
Autistic people help develop this way

384
00:26:09,240 --> 00:26:10,880
of understanding attention.

385
00:26:10,880 --> 00:26:15,080
Deep interests can offer learning, memory
and joy.

386
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:17,400
When your child is absorbed in building,

387
00:26:17,400 --> 00:26:20,560
reading about space or listening to a
favourite song,

388
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:23,200
their attention may be deeply invested.

389
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:27,120
Asking them to stop for dinner can involve
real effort.

390
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:29,680
Warning, time and a helpful transition

391
00:26:29,680 --> 00:26:31,280
can make a difference.

392
00:26:31,280 --> 00:26:34,440
That absorbed state, sometimes called
flow,

393
00:26:34,440 --> 00:26:36,280
is also something worth protecting.

394
00:26:38,520 --> 00:26:42,590
Executive functioning is a name for
processes we use to organise and

395
00:26:42,590 --> 00:26:44,440
carry out everyday tasks.

396
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:48,630
The slide includes planning, organisation,
time management and working

397
00:26:48,630 --> 00:26:49,000
memory.

398
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:53,880
It also includes thinking about our
thinking, managing emotions,

399
00:26:53,880 --> 00:26:54,920
flexibility,

400
00:26:54,920 --> 00:26:58,800
attention, getting started and persisting.
We use different

401
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:01,560
combinations of these throughout the day.

402
00:27:01,560 --> 00:27:07,660
When a task is hard, identifying the
particular demand can help us find

403
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:08,200
useful support.

404
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:12,450
Let's look at getting started and
switching focus, because they ask

405
00:27:12,450 --> 00:27:14,600
different things of a person.

406
00:27:16,320 --> 00:27:19,440
Getting ready for school can sound like
one instruction, but it

407
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:21,600
contains many steps.

408
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:24,520
Where do I start? What comes next?

409
00:27:24,520 --> 00:27:27,080
What needs to be ready at the end?

410
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:30,760
If the whole task isn't clear, the first
step may be difficult to find.

411
00:27:30,760 --> 00:27:34,320
A visible sequence or showing the first
step can help make the task

412
00:27:34,320 --> 00:27:35,760
more understandable.

413
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:40,480
Before adding another reminder, ask which
part needs to become clearer.

414
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:44,240
Switching focus is a different demand.

415
00:27:44,240 --> 00:27:47,680
A child deeply absorbed in play may need
time to disengage

416
00:27:47,680 --> 00:27:50,480
before they can take up the next activity.

417
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:53,040
An unexpected interruption can feel much
bigger

418
00:27:53,040 --> 00:27:55,800
than it looks from the outside.

419
00:27:55,800 --> 00:28:00,120
Think back to the torch beam. A warning
that means what it says,

420
00:28:00,120 --> 00:28:04,360
time to finish a small part, and a clear
sense of what comes next

421
00:28:04,360 --> 00:28:06,920
can support a transition.

422
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:10,080
Check what works for your child.

423
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:15,450
This slide is reserved for a young
person's account of deep focus and

424
00:28:15,450 --> 00:28:16,680
transitions.

425
00:28:16,680 --> 00:28:21,350
The supplied workshop materials still show
a video placeholder here, so

426
00:28:21,350 --> 00:28:21,760
there isn't a

427
00:28:21,760 --> 00:28:24,160
film to play in this walkthrough.

428
00:28:24,160 --> 00:28:27,560
We'll move into the reflection using the
ideas we've just explored,

429
00:28:27,560 --> 00:28:28,640
while keeping that space

430
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:31,040
for a first-person account when the clip
is available.

431
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:36,980
Turn to page seven. What are your child's
deep interests? What do you

432
00:28:36,980 --> 00:28:38,800
notice when they're fully engaged?

433
00:28:38,800 --> 00:28:41,800
When is it difficult to get started or
shift attention?

434
00:28:41,800 --> 00:28:45,000
Think of a time they were completely
absorbed at home. What were they

435
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:45,300
doing?

436
00:28:45,300 --> 00:28:49,050
Choose the questions that feel useful.
Begin with a real moment, then

437
00:28:49,050 --> 00:28:51,200
talk with your table or write privately.

438
00:28:51,200 --> 00:28:55,000
Keep enjoyment and strengths alongside the
difficult transitions.

439
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
Pause now for your discussion.

440
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:09,680
As you return, notice the difference
between getting started, staying

441
00:29:09,680 --> 00:29:10,680
absorbed and switching

442
00:29:10,680 --> 00:29:11,680
away.

443
00:29:11,680 --> 00:29:14,760
Each may call for a different kind of
support.

444
00:29:14,760 --> 00:29:17,980
Naming what you see gives you something
specific to explore with your

445
00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:18,240
child.

446
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:21,600
Keep the interest itself in the picture as
a source of enjoyment.

447
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:26,960
Let's return to interoception, the body's
inside sense that we named

448
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:27,320
earlier.

449
00:29:27,320 --> 00:29:30,520
Internal signals add another part to the
picture.

450
00:29:30,520 --> 00:29:35,610
Some experiences are hard to notice or
describe even when they strongly

451
00:29:35,610 --> 00:29:37,300
affect how a person feels.

452
00:29:40,040 --> 00:29:44,070
Interoception concerns signals from inside
the body: temperature,

453
00:29:44,070 --> 00:29:46,260
hunger, thirst or needing the bathroom.

454
00:29:46,260 --> 00:29:51,620
Alexithymia describes difficulty
identifying and describing emotions

455
00:29:51,620 --> 00:29:52,880
and feelings.

456
00:29:52,880 --> 00:29:57,780
They're related ideas, but noticing a body
signal and knowing how to

457
00:29:57,780 --> 00:30:00,060
describe an emotion are different tasks.

458
00:30:00,060 --> 00:30:05,020
A child might notice hunger only when it
becomes intense, or find it

459
00:30:05,020 --> 00:30:09,290
hard to tell whether a fluttery feeling
before a party is excitement or

460
00:30:09,290 --> 00:30:09,940
worry.

461
00:30:10,860 --> 00:30:12,920
These examples give us questions to
explore,

462
00:30:12,920 --> 00:30:17,280
rather than a diagnosis. We can offer time
and ways to communicate what

463
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:21,420
is happening inside, without insisting on
an immediate answer.

464
00:30:23,920 --> 00:30:27,710
Sometimes a build-up is happening inside
even when the surface doesn't

465
00:30:27,710 --> 00:30:28,080
show it.

466
00:30:28,080 --> 00:30:33,080
Early signs may be subtle, individual or
simply unavailable to us.

467
00:30:33,080 --> 00:30:37,040
This is an invitation to learn together,
never a reason to blame

468
00:30:37,040 --> 00:30:39,040
yourself for not noticing sooner.

469
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:42,880
Over time, your child and the people who
know them may build a shared

470
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:44,440
picture of what helps.

471
00:30:44,440 --> 00:30:49,160
Describing changes clearly can help family
members and school respond

472
00:30:49,160 --> 00:30:50,320
more consistently.

473
00:30:51,100 --> 00:30:54,050
The picture should stay open to what your
child communicates and to

474
00:30:54,050 --> 00:30:55,780
things we haven't understood yet.

475
00:30:58,880 --> 00:31:02,440
Stay on page seven for Reading the signs.
What changes do you notice before

476
00:31:02,440 --> 00:31:03,920
your child becomes overwhelmed?

477
00:31:03,920 --> 00:31:07,720
Describe what you can see or hear, as
specifically as you can.

478
00:31:07,720 --> 00:31:12,080
What would be useful for another carer or
school to know?

479
00:31:12,080 --> 00:31:15,000
You might also write what you aren't sure
about yet.

480
00:31:15,000 --> 00:31:18,640
Take a little private thinking time, then
share only if you wish.

481
00:31:18,640 --> 00:31:21,160
Pause the video while you make those
notes.

482
00:31:29,760 --> 00:31:33,040
Keep that description available for our
work on school partnership.

483
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:37,160
A useful note helps someone recognise a
change and respond with support.

484
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:40,950
It doesn't need to explain everything, and
it can change as you learn

485
00:31:40,950 --> 00:31:41,360
more.

486
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:45,160
We've now considered sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals

487
00:31:45,160 --> 00:31:46,480
as connected parts of

488
00:31:46,480 --> 00:31:47,600
the same person's day.

489
00:31:50,280 --> 00:31:52,120
That's our second session.

490
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,360
After morning tea, we'll continue with
communication, connection and

491
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:57,320
managing daily capacity.

492
00:31:57,320 --> 00:31:59,640
Take a break now if you need one.

493
00:31:59,640 --> 00:32:03,160
When you return, bring the same child to
mind, along with the strengths

494
00:32:03,160 --> 00:32:05,000
and questions you've been gathering.

495
00:32:11,680 --> 00:32:12,680
Welcome back.

496
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:16,680
This session continues the picture we've
been building.

497
00:32:16,680 --> 00:32:19,680
Sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals can all shape

498
00:32:19,680 --> 00:32:22,240
communication, connection and

499
00:32:22,240 --> 00:32:24,880
the energy available across a day.

500
00:32:24,880 --> 00:32:28,080
Keep your own child in mind as we explore
these together.

501
00:32:30,440 --> 00:32:33,320
Let's begin with communication.

502
00:32:33,320 --> 00:32:35,800
Before thinking about anyone else's way of
communicating,

503
00:32:35,800 --> 00:32:37,320
we'll notice how many different ways

504
00:32:37,320 --> 00:32:38,880
we already use ourselves.

505
00:32:41,520 --> 00:32:44,800
Since you woke up today, how have you
communicated?

506
00:32:44,800 --> 00:32:46,720
Think of three different ways.

507
00:32:46,720 --> 00:32:49,640
It could be a spoken greeting, a message,
a wave,

508
00:32:49,640 --> 00:32:51,560
a look, pointing to something,

509
00:32:51,560 --> 00:32:54,120
or taking a moment before responding.

510
00:32:54,120 --> 00:32:55,560
Have a quiet think,

511
00:32:55,560 --> 00:32:58,400
then share one example if you'd like to.

512
00:32:58,400 --> 00:32:59,640
Pause here for a moment.

513
00:33:07,800 --> 00:33:11,400
We all move between different ways of
communicating.

514
00:33:11,400 --> 00:33:13,520
Speech is one part of that picture.

515
00:33:13,520 --> 00:33:19,310
Writing, gesture, a device, showing a
photo or moving away can carry a

516
00:33:19,310 --> 00:33:20,800
message too.

517
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:23,730
Keep that variety in mind when we consider
what helps someone

518
00:33:23,730 --> 00:33:26,420
understand and express themselves.

519
00:33:27,960 --> 00:33:31,800
The two sides of this slide separate
understanding, information coming

520
00:33:31,800 --> 00:33:33,320
in, from expressing,

521
00:33:33,320 --> 00:33:36,680
messages going out. How much someone
speaks doesn't tell us how much

522
00:33:36,680 --> 00:33:38,400
they understand.

523
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:42,530
A person may speak fluently and still need
support to understand spoken

524
00:33:42,530 --> 00:33:43,560
information.

525
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:47,960
Someone else may understand more than they
can readily express.

526
00:33:47,960 --> 00:33:52,120
A A C means augmentative and alternative
communication.

527
00:33:52,120 --> 00:33:56,370
It can include signs, a communication
board or book, typing or a

528
00:33:56,370 --> 00:33:58,200
communication device.

529
00:33:58,200 --> 00:34:02,360
Some people use it alongside speech, and
some instead of speech.

530
00:34:02,360 --> 00:34:05,820
Communication can change with the setting,
the demands and how someone

531
00:34:05,820 --> 00:34:06,760
is feeling.

532
00:34:06,760 --> 00:34:10,600
Repeated words or familiar scripts can
carry meaning too.

533
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:13,080
Stay curious about their purpose and the
context.

534
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:17,560
As you watch this video, notice what
supports communication.

535
00:34:17,560 --> 00:34:19,880
Choose one idea that stands out:

536
00:34:19,880 --> 00:34:22,560
perhaps a communication method, something
another person does,

537
00:34:22,560 --> 00:34:24,600
or something about the environment.

538
00:34:24,600 --> 00:34:26,360
Let's hear the speakers' own accounts.

539
00:40:00,840 --> 00:40:04,440
What stood out to you about what supported
communication?

540
00:40:04,440 --> 00:40:06,600
Take a moment to think, or share one
observation

541
00:40:06,600 --> 00:40:07,920
with someone nearby.

542
00:40:07,920 --> 00:40:10,320
Stay close to what the speakers said.

543
00:40:10,320 --> 00:40:11,800
Pause here if you'd like longer.

544
00:40:19,880 --> 00:40:22,280
Keep that observation with you.

545
00:40:22,280 --> 00:40:25,800
There is no single Autistic communication
profile.

546
00:40:25,800 --> 00:40:28,360
We'll now consider the people and places
in your child's own

547
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:29,400
communication world.

548
00:40:32,400 --> 00:40:36,000
Turn to the communication network on page
nine.

549
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:39,570
Your child is in the centre. Around them
are home, school, friends,

550
00:40:39,570 --> 00:40:41,520
community and supports.

551
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:43,800
Add one person or place now.

552
00:40:43,800 --> 00:40:46,800
You can write, draw or use a few words.

553
00:40:46,800 --> 00:40:49,240
There is no need to fill every box.

554
00:40:49,240 --> 00:40:53,240
A network can be small, changing or still
developing.

555
00:40:53,240 --> 00:40:57,040
A grandparent, teacher or coach may see
different parts of

556
00:40:57,040 --> 00:40:58,320
communication.

557
00:40:58,320 --> 00:41:00,720
Pause while you add one starting point.

558
00:41:09,680 --> 00:41:12,680
You can return to the rest later,
including with your child and other

559
00:41:12,680 --> 00:41:14,480
communication partners.

560
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:19,450
This page helps us notice who is connected
and what they might need to

561
00:41:19,450 --> 00:41:19,920
understand.

562
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:23,440
There is no ranking of who knows your
child best.

563
00:41:25,080 --> 00:41:28,480
On the same page, let's think about what
useful knowledge could travel

564
00:41:28,480 --> 00:41:29,960
between people and places.

565
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:32,360
Purple is a communication strength.

566
00:41:32,360 --> 00:41:35,200
Blue is something that helps
communication.

567
00:41:35,200 --> 00:41:37,700
Orange is something you would like others
to know.

568
00:41:37,700 --> 00:41:41,700
Use coloured notes if you have them, or
the three workbook boxes.

569
00:41:41,700 --> 00:41:43,600
Write one, two or all three.

570
00:41:43,600 --> 00:41:47,660
You can use a made-up example, and you can
keep your notes private.

571
00:41:47,660 --> 00:41:51,840
If you share at a table or on a board,
leave out identifying details.

572
00:41:51,840 --> 00:41:54,476
Pause now for writing and optional
sharing.

573
00:42:02,480 --> 00:42:04,400
Look across those ideas.

574
00:42:04,400 --> 00:42:07,920
What strengths might be missed if we
looked only for speech?

575
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:12,120
Which supports involve changing what a
communication partner does or

576
00:42:12,120 --> 00:42:13,480
provides?

577
00:42:13,480 --> 00:42:18,360
Keep a blue note, or something from the
What helps box, in mind.

578
00:42:18,360 --> 00:42:20,480
That's our link to the next slide.

579
00:42:20,480 --> 00:42:24,000
Useful information can help a child avoid
having to start again with

580
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:24,960
every new person.

581
00:42:27,600 --> 00:42:30,840
Here are three options to consider
alongside what you've written.

582
00:42:30,840 --> 00:42:35,890
Clear communication means familiar,
specific words and one idea or

583
00:42:35,890 --> 00:42:37,800
step at a time.

584
00:42:37,800 --> 00:42:41,320
Processing time means allowing space to
understand,

585
00:42:41,320 --> 00:42:44,600
organise a response and communicate it.

586
00:42:44,600 --> 00:42:48,680
Visual supports mean showing information
as well as saying it.

587
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:51,720
The useful support depends on the person
and the moment.

588
00:42:51,720 --> 00:42:55,480
Try leaving a pause without filling it
with more words.

589
00:42:55,480 --> 00:42:58,600
Keep the person's communication methods
available,

590
00:42:58,600 --> 00:43:01,120
including when the message is no.

591
00:43:01,120 --> 00:43:04,000
These are ways to make communication more
accessible,

592
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:06,080
and we need to check whether they help.

593
00:43:07,840 --> 00:43:11,680
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to.

594
00:43:11,680 --> 00:43:15,240
On this slide, a timer answers how much
time is left.

595
00:43:15,240 --> 00:43:18,040
A sequence answers what happens next.

596
00:43:18,040 --> 00:43:20,400
A daily schedule could answer what is
happening today,

597
00:43:20,400 --> 00:43:24,360
although that example space is blank in
the supplied slide.

598
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:27,080
You might already use a shopping list,
calendar

599
00:43:27,080 --> 00:43:28,880
or reminder on your phone.

600
00:43:28,880 --> 00:43:30,920
What visual information do you use at
home,

601
00:43:30,920 --> 00:43:32,200
and what makes it helpful?

602
00:43:32,200 --> 00:43:35,120
Share a brief example if you wish,

603
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:36,960
or note one privately.

604
00:43:36,980 --> 00:43:38,060
Pause here.

605
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:49,480
The helpful format differs from person to
person.

606
00:43:49,480 --> 00:43:53,340
It might be an object, photo, symbol or
written words, on paper or a

607
00:43:53,340 --> 00:43:54,640
device.

608
00:43:54,640 --> 00:43:58,600
Check that the information makes sense and
is available when needed.

609
00:43:58,600 --> 00:44:02,200
A visual should offer information.
Communication tools still need to

610
00:44:02,200 --> 00:44:03,840
stay available in their own right.

611
00:44:06,040 --> 00:44:09,360
Connection involves understanding in both
directions.

612
00:44:09,360 --> 00:44:13,220
The double empathy idea, introduced by
Autistic researcher

613
00:44:13,220 --> 00:44:16,500
Damian Milton, asks us to consider
misunderstandings

614
00:44:16,500 --> 00:44:19,040
between people with different experiences

615
00:44:19,040 --> 00:44:22,500
and communication styles as a shared gap.

616
00:44:22,500 --> 00:44:24,460
The image shows two people sending

617
00:44:24,460 --> 00:44:26,660
and receiving in their own ways.

618
00:44:26,660 --> 00:44:29,340
Both have something to understand.

619
00:44:29,340 --> 00:44:30,740
You may already do this at home

620
00:44:30,740 --> 00:44:32,660
as you learn your child's communication

621
00:44:32,660 --> 00:44:34,540
and they learn yours.

622
00:44:34,540 --> 00:44:37,460
We can ask what we, as communication
partners,

623
00:44:37,460 --> 00:44:39,740
might change to make the exchange easier.

624
00:44:39,740 --> 00:44:43,500
Listening and adjustment belong on both
sides.

625
00:44:45,600 --> 00:44:49,720
Let's hear one person's experience of
being social.

626
00:44:49,720 --> 00:44:53,020
As you listen, notice what connection
means in this account, without

627
00:44:53,020 --> 00:44:54,280
expecting it to describe

628
00:44:54,280 --> 00:44:56,977
every Autistic person's experience.

629
00:45:44,040 --> 00:45:45,540
Take a quiet moment.

630
00:45:45,540 --> 00:45:46,900
What did you notice?

631
00:45:46,900 --> 00:45:49,760
You can share one reflection or hold it
privately.

632
00:45:49,760 --> 00:45:51,780
Pause the video if you need longer.

633
00:45:59,800 --> 00:46:03,000
Carry that reflection into the next
discussion.

634
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:06,000
Connection can take different forms, and
the conditions around it

635
00:46:06,000 --> 00:46:07,800
can make a difference.

636
00:46:09,360 --> 00:46:12,950
Social connection can happen in family
life, school, the community and

637
00:46:12,950 --> 00:46:13,420
online.

638
00:46:13,420 --> 00:46:18,180
It may involve conversation, shared
interests, doing something

639
00:46:18,180 --> 00:46:21,450
alongside another person, quiet company or
contact that happens only

640
00:46:21,450 --> 00:46:22,500
occasionally.

641
00:46:22,500 --> 00:46:25,870
Sharing lots of detail about an interest
can itself be a way of

642
00:46:25,870 --> 00:46:27,620
reaching towards someone.

643
00:46:27,620 --> 00:46:32,200
Families and cultures also have different
ways of being together.

644
00:46:32,200 --> 00:46:35,660
Think about the connections your child
already enjoys.

645
00:46:36,380 --> 00:46:39,920
We can look for what feels meaningful and
workable for them, rather than

646
00:46:39,920 --> 00:46:43,140
using one style of interaction as the
measure for everyone.

647
00:46:46,320 --> 00:46:49,280
On page ten, think about when your child
seems most comfortable

648
00:46:49,280 --> 00:46:50,520
connecting.

649
00:46:50,520 --> 00:46:51,940
What do they enjoy:

650
00:46:51,940 --> 00:46:56,560
talking, gaming, sport, making something,
shared interests, or being

651
00:46:56,560 --> 00:46:58,240
together quietly?

652
00:46:58,240 --> 00:47:01,570
When does it feel enjoyable, and when does
it become tiring or

653
00:47:01,570 --> 00:47:02,800
overwhelming?

654
00:47:02,800 --> 00:47:05,830
Then consider what adults could change to
make connections safer,

655
00:47:05,830 --> 00:47:08,500
easier or more meaningful.

656
00:47:08,500 --> 00:47:10,220
Start with your child's preferences.

657
00:47:10,220 --> 00:47:13,560
You can reflect privately or talk with
your table.

658
00:47:13,560 --> 00:47:15,760
Pause for the discussion.

659
00:47:23,840 --> 00:47:27,240
As we return, keep one condition that
seems to help in mind.

660
00:47:27,240 --> 00:47:30,560
It could be the person, place, activity or
amount of time.

661
00:47:30,560 --> 00:47:34,110
Some children have few connections right
now, and this is not a

662
00:47:34,110 --> 00:47:35,440
comparison between families.

663
00:47:35,440 --> 00:47:39,090
Meaningful connection can look different
across people, settings and

664
00:47:39,090 --> 00:47:39,640
days.

665
00:47:41,720 --> 00:47:43,520
Now let's look at daily capacity.

666
00:47:43,520 --> 00:47:46,260
Something can be enjoyable and still take
energy.

667
00:47:46,260 --> 00:47:48,560
Communication, sensory processing,

668
00:47:48,560 --> 00:47:50,560
transitions and social connection

669
00:47:50,560 --> 00:47:53,460
all draw on the resources available in
that moment.

670
00:47:55,280 --> 00:47:58,440
Think of energy as a battery. Each day can
begin with a different

671
00:47:58,440 --> 00:48:00,980
amount of charge, and activities draw on
it.

672
00:48:00,980 --> 00:48:04,380
When it runs low, ordinary things can
become harder.

673
00:48:04,380 --> 00:48:08,640
Many people also use the spoons analogy: a
limited supply of energy,

674
00:48:08,640 --> 00:48:11,640
with different activities using different
amounts.

675
00:48:11,640 --> 00:48:16,480
These are optional ways to describe an
experience, rather than measures

676
00:48:16,480 --> 00:48:17,520
of ability.

677
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:22,500
The important question is what costs
energy and what helps restore it.

678
00:48:23,340 --> 00:48:26,930
Rest, familiar interests or a
better-fitting environment may help

679
00:48:26,930 --> 00:48:27,700
recharge.

680
00:48:27,700 --> 00:48:32,030
Support needs to match the charge that is
actually available, not the

681
00:48:32,030 --> 00:48:34,200
amount we hoped would be there.

682
00:48:36,080 --> 00:48:39,920
Capacity can vary across a day and from
one day to another.

683
00:48:39,920 --> 00:48:46,170
Stress, sensory demands, sleep, social
situations and other events can

684
00:48:46,170 --> 00:48:48,440
change what feels manageable.

685
00:48:48,440 --> 00:48:52,200
The same request may be easy on one
occasion and too much on another.

686
00:48:52,200 --> 00:48:55,910
Think about what seems to use your child's
energy and what lightens the

687
00:48:55,910 --> 00:48:56,160
load.

688
00:48:56,160 --> 00:48:59,640
We don't need to interpret a change in
capacity as unwillingness.

689
00:48:59,640 --> 00:49:03,630
It gives us a reason to look again at the
demands and the support

690
00:49:03,630 --> 00:49:04,480
available.

691
00:49:06,640 --> 00:49:09,980
Masking can place another demand on
energy.

692
00:49:09,980 --> 00:49:13,000
We'll hear a first-person account now.

693
00:49:13,000 --> 00:49:15,180
Let the speaker describe their own
experience,

694
00:49:15,180 --> 00:49:18,900
and leave a little room afterwards to
reflect.

695
00:50:51,360 --> 00:50:53,360
Take a moment before we move on.

696
00:50:56,480 --> 00:50:59,360
The image places two moments beside one
another:

697
00:50:59,360 --> 00:51:03,820
managing at school, and arriving home with
very little energy left.

698
00:51:03,820 --> 00:51:07,200
Appearing fine may not show the effort
underneath.

699
00:51:07,200 --> 00:51:10,880
That doesn't tell us exactly what is
happening for every child.

700
00:51:10,880 --> 00:51:15,340
It reminds us to consider what a day
costs, including effort we may not

701
00:51:15,340 --> 00:51:16,120
see.

702
00:51:16,120 --> 00:51:20,050
Changes to the environment and
expectations can sometimes reduce that

703
00:51:20,050 --> 00:51:20,720
effort.

704
00:51:20,720 --> 00:51:23,920
We'll return to the after-school
experience in our next session.

705
00:51:25,440 --> 00:51:29,280
On page ten, consider where your child
seems to work hard to appear fine,

706
00:51:29,280 --> 00:51:32,440
and where they seem most comfortable being
themselves.

707
00:51:32,440 --> 00:51:36,240
What could adults change to help them feel
more supported?

708
00:51:36,240 --> 00:51:38,940
Start with private thinking.

709
00:51:38,940 --> 00:51:42,980
You do not need to prove masking is
occurring, or share a personal story.

710
00:51:42,980 --> 00:51:45,940
Keep your notes descriptive and your
questions open.

711
00:51:45,940 --> 00:51:49,980
If you wish, discuss one practical change
with your table.

712
00:51:49,980 --> 00:51:52,096
Pause here.

713
00:52:00,120 --> 00:52:03,280
Keep that possible change in mind.

714
00:52:03,280 --> 00:52:07,000
Recognising something now doesn't mean you
should have known it earlier.

715
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:10,200
We can learn from new understanding
without blame.

716
00:52:10,200 --> 00:52:14,200
Making room for a person to be themselves
connects directly to the idea

717
00:52:14,200 --> 00:52:15,560
on our closing slide.

718
00:52:18,600 --> 00:52:22,040
Across these two sessions, we've been
building a picture of your child.

719
00:52:22,040 --> 00:52:26,430
How the world arrives through their
senses. How they notice detail,

720
00:52:26,430 --> 00:52:30,880
focus deeply and bring particular
strengths. How they experience

721
00:52:30,880 --> 00:52:35,040
internal signals, communicate and reach
towards other people.

722
00:52:35,040 --> 00:52:39,340
Managing those demands every day can take
a great deal of energy,

723
00:52:39,340 --> 00:52:41,680
especially when the surroundings don't
fit.

724
00:52:42,720 --> 00:52:47,610
Sensory load, transitions, communication
across different styles, and

725
00:52:47,610 --> 00:52:51,040
sometimes appearing fine can all add to
the cost.

726
00:52:51,040 --> 00:52:55,350
A child who knows who they are, and feels
valued for it, has more room

727
00:52:55,350 --> 00:52:59,020
for learning, connection and
self-understanding.

728
00:52:59,020 --> 00:53:00,860
Belonging matters.

729
00:53:00,860 --> 00:53:05,170
So does genuine choice about how they
communicate, where they feel

730
00:53:05,170 --> 00:53:07,020
comfortable and when they need a break.

731
00:53:07,020 --> 00:53:11,310
Knowing other Autistic people can offer
connection and a sense of

732
00:53:11,310 --> 00:53:12,320
shared experience.

733
00:53:13,440 --> 00:53:16,520
Think back to the strengths and joys you
wrote down this morning.

734
00:53:16,520 --> 00:53:20,380
Supporting a positive Autistic identity
can begin with listening,

735
00:53:20,380 --> 00:53:24,200
making room for those strengths, and
showing that this whole person is

736
00:53:24,200 --> 00:53:25,340
worth knowing.

737
00:53:25,340 --> 00:53:27,560
We'll pause here.

738
00:53:27,560 --> 00:53:29,500
Take your lunch break when you're ready.

739
00:53:29,500 --> 00:53:32,300
When we return, we'll consider what this
understanding can look like at

740
00:53:32,300 --> 00:53:33,520
home and in the community.

741
00:53:45,200 --> 00:53:49,230
Welcome back. This morning was about
understanding Autistic experience.

742
00:53:49,230 --> 00:53:50,640
Now we consider what that

743
00:53:50,640 --> 00:53:54,810
understanding can mean in everyday family
life. You know your family

744
00:53:54,810 --> 00:53:56,720
best. Take the ideas that

745
00:53:56,720 --> 00:54:00,800
fit and leave those that don't. We'll look
at distress and recovery,

746
00:54:00,800 --> 00:54:01,840
familiar routines,

747
00:54:01,840 --> 00:54:05,120
interests, outings and the needs of the
whole family.

748
00:54:05,120 --> 00:54:10,430
We are looking for a manageable next step,
with your child's preferences

749
00:54:10,430 --> 00:54:12,320
and capacity at the centre.

750
00:54:13,840 --> 00:54:16,680
What we can see and hear may be only the
tip of the iceberg.

751
00:54:16,680 --> 00:54:21,220
Underneath can sit thoughts, feelings,
values and needs,

752
00:54:21,220 --> 00:54:25,240
including belonging, predictability,
security and self-expression.

753
00:54:25,240 --> 00:54:28,380
Think privately of a hard moment.

754
00:54:28,380 --> 00:54:30,680
What had happened beforehand?

755
00:54:30,680 --> 00:54:35,360
Perhaps there was an unexpected change, a
noisy shop,

756
00:54:35,360 --> 00:54:38,960
or a request that was simply too big for
that day.

757
00:54:38,960 --> 00:54:42,240
We may not know the answer straight away.

758
00:54:43,160 --> 00:54:46,330
Behaviour can give us information, and
curiosity helps us look beyond

759
00:54:46,330 --> 00:54:47,140
the surface.

760
00:54:47,140 --> 00:54:50,620
This isn't about blaming a child or a
parent.

761
00:54:50,620 --> 00:54:53,960
It is about understanding the conditions
around a difficult moment.

762
00:54:56,560 --> 00:54:59,000
Overwhelm can turn inward or outward.

763
00:54:59,000 --> 00:55:02,040
A shutdown may look like becoming very
still, freezing,

764
00:55:02,040 --> 00:55:04,000
withdrawing or being less able to speak

765
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:05,800
and explain what is needed.

766
00:55:05,800 --> 00:55:08,840
A meltdown may involve crying, running
away

767
00:55:08,840 --> 00:55:12,120
or a large outward expression of distress.

768
00:55:12,120 --> 00:55:15,320
These are involuntary responses to
overwhelm.

769
00:55:15,320 --> 00:55:17,520
Quietness can be easy to miss,

770
00:55:17,520 --> 00:55:19,880
especially when someone seems to be
managing.

771
00:55:19,880 --> 00:55:22,640
The response alone may not tell us the
full story.

772
00:55:22,640 --> 00:55:25,600
Keep the person's safety and need for
reduced demands

773
00:55:25,600 --> 00:55:29,360
in view, and allow some space around these
experiences.

774
00:55:29,360 --> 00:55:31,760
They can be hard for families to revisit.

775
00:55:33,640 --> 00:55:36,320
Sometimes overload builds over a long
period.

776
00:55:36,320 --> 00:55:39,400
Autistic burnout describes deep physical,
mental

777
00:55:39,400 --> 00:55:41,840
and emotional exhaustion, with a reduction

778
00:55:41,840 --> 00:55:44,200
in capacity that may last.

779
00:55:44,200 --> 00:55:46,320
School Can't describes being unable to
attend

780
00:55:46,320 --> 00:55:48,120
because school has become overwhelming

781
00:55:48,120 --> 00:55:52,040
through anxiety, fear or sensory load.

782
00:55:52,040 --> 00:55:54,680
These terms help us consider what someone
may have been

783
00:55:54,680 --> 00:55:58,160
carrying and what support or recovery they
need.

784
00:55:58,160 --> 00:56:00,560
They don't let us diagnose an individual
situation

785
00:56:00,560 --> 00:56:01,880
from a slide.

786
00:56:01,880 --> 00:56:05,520
If your family is living through this, it
can be very hard.

787
00:56:05,520 --> 00:56:07,920
A conversation with appropriate health and
school supports

788
00:56:07,920 --> 00:56:10,840
can help explore the individual picture.

789
00:56:10,840 --> 00:56:12,920
We'll look at working with school in
Session Five.

790
00:56:14,440 --> 00:56:17,850
In the moment, try reducing input: fewer
words, fewer questions and

791
00:56:17,850 --> 00:56:18,660
less noise.

792
00:56:18,660 --> 00:56:22,650
A quiet, safe space works best when you've
talked about it together

793
00:56:22,650 --> 00:56:24,780
beforehand, while everyone is calm.

794
00:56:24,780 --> 00:56:28,120
Keep your approach matched to what you
know helps your child.

795
00:56:28,120 --> 00:56:31,880
A distressed person may have very little
capacity for explanation or

796
00:56:31,880 --> 00:56:33,060
problem-solving.

797
00:56:33,060 --> 00:56:36,400
The conversation about what happened can
wait until they are ready.

798
00:56:36,400 --> 00:56:40,810
On page twelve, there's room to gather
early signs, what helps in the

799
00:56:40,810 --> 00:56:43,060
moment and what helps recovery.

800
00:56:43,180 --> 00:56:44,940
You can add to that picture over time.

801
00:56:44,940 --> 00:56:48,690
These are options to consider, rather than
a script every family has to

802
00:56:48,690 --> 00:56:49,360
follow.

803
00:56:51,400 --> 00:56:54,970
Co-regulation means a trusted adult
helping a child settle through a

804
00:56:54,970 --> 00:56:57,000
steady, responsive presence.

805
00:56:57,000 --> 00:57:00,040
Sometimes that involves very few words.

806
00:57:00,040 --> 00:57:04,760
Self-regulation is what a person does to
manage their own energy and

807
00:57:04,760 --> 00:57:05,200
feelings,

808
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:07,360
and it develops with support over time.

809
00:57:07,360 --> 00:57:11,240
Nobody manages to be calm every time.

810
00:57:11,240 --> 00:57:15,160
If you're exhausted or upset yourself,
this can be hard.

811
00:57:15,160 --> 00:57:18,640
The point is to notice the support both of
you need, and to make room

812
00:57:18,640 --> 00:57:20,040
for it where possible.

813
00:57:20,720 --> 00:57:23,520
We'll return to your own battery later in
this session.

814
00:57:25,720 --> 00:57:28,700
Some families recognise an after-school
crash.

815
00:57:28,700 --> 00:57:32,460
A child may appear to manage throughout
school, then have very little

816
00:57:32,460 --> 00:57:33,520
left when they get home.

817
00:57:33,520 --> 00:57:38,100
What school sees and what you see can both
be real parts of the day.

818
00:57:38,100 --> 00:57:43,400
A softer landing might mean food, quiet,
movement or time with a screen

819
00:57:43,400 --> 00:57:47,000
or favourite activity, guided by what
helps your child recover.

820
00:57:47,000 --> 00:57:51,300
Questions about the day, and homework, can
wait.

821
00:57:51,300 --> 00:57:55,300
A familiar after-school routine can make
that recovery predictable.

822
00:57:56,280 --> 00:58:00,020
Your child may talk later, perhaps at
bedtime, or may not want to talk

823
00:58:00,020 --> 00:58:01,160
about school.

824
00:58:01,160 --> 00:58:03,000
Notice what helps them settle.

825
00:58:03,000 --> 00:58:06,740
What happens at home can give school
useful information about the

826
00:58:06,740 --> 00:58:07,800
overall load.

827
00:58:09,600 --> 00:58:13,320
Predictability can reduce uncertainty, and
it doesn't require turning

828
00:58:13,320 --> 00:58:15,120
home into a classroom.

829
00:58:15,120 --> 00:58:18,960
Choose one or two routines that would help
your family most.

830
00:58:18,960 --> 00:58:24,160
Perhaps mornings, after school or bedtime
need a clearer sequence.

831
00:58:24,160 --> 00:58:28,800
A schedule on the fridge or a now-and-next
card may help where it makes

832
00:58:28,800 --> 00:58:29,760
information easier

833
00:58:29,760 --> 00:58:30,760
to understand.

834
00:58:30,760 --> 00:58:35,320
If you give a five-minute warning, try to
make it mean five minutes.

835
00:58:35,320 --> 00:58:39,670
When plans change, name the change, show
it if helpful and give as much

836
00:58:39,670 --> 00:58:40,560
warning as you

837
00:58:40,560 --> 00:58:42,200
can.

838
00:58:42,200 --> 00:58:44,440
The routine should serve your family.

839
00:58:44,440 --> 00:58:47,670
If it creates more work and stress than it
solves, it needs another

840
00:58:47,670 --> 00:58:48,080
look.

841
00:58:50,600 --> 00:58:54,560
Interests can be an anchor for regulation,
connection and joy.

842
00:58:54,560 --> 00:58:58,640
Time with a familiar interest may help
restore energy.

843
00:58:58,640 --> 00:59:02,080
It can also offer a way into your child's
world:

844
00:59:02,080 --> 00:59:04,320
listening while they explain,

845
00:59:04,320 --> 00:59:07,800
watching what they make or joining in when
invited.

846
00:59:07,800 --> 00:59:11,840
An interest is part of their experience
and identity.

847
00:59:11,840 --> 00:59:14,360
Taking it away as a consequence can also
take away

848
00:59:14,360 --> 00:59:15,680
a source of regulation.

849
00:59:15,680 --> 00:59:19,000
If that approach has been familiar at
home,

850
00:59:19,000 --> 00:59:21,280
you can consider the function the interest
serves

851
00:59:21,280 --> 00:59:23,960
and what another response might look like.

852
00:59:23,960 --> 00:59:26,520
Stay curious about what it gives your
child.

853
00:59:28,640 --> 00:59:30,960
Turn to Planning an outing on page twelve.

854
00:59:30,960 --> 00:59:33,360
Choose something familiar or coming up:

855
00:59:33,360 --> 00:59:35,880
a party, a shopping trip or a swimming
lesson.

856
00:59:35,880 --> 00:59:38,160
Think about before, during and after.

857
00:59:38,160 --> 00:59:40,760
Beforehand, what can you preview?

858
00:59:40,760 --> 00:59:42,220
Where are we going,

859
00:59:42,220 --> 00:59:43,300
what will happen,

860
00:59:43,300 --> 00:59:44,740
how long might it be,

861
00:59:44,740 --> 00:59:46,280
and what if it is too much?

862
00:59:46,280 --> 00:59:50,640
During the outing, how will you notice
energy and use an exit plan?

863
00:59:50,640 --> 00:59:53,700
Afterwards, what recovery time might be
needed?

864
00:59:53,700 --> 00:59:56,620
Share one idea at your table if you wish,

865
00:59:56,620 --> 00:59:59,400
and add any useful ideas to your notes.

866
00:59:59,400 --> 01:00:02,760
If outings aren't workable for your family
right now,

867
01:00:02,760 --> 01:00:05,640
you can choose a smaller situation or
simply listen.

868
01:00:05,640 --> 01:00:08,420
Pause here for the activity.

869
01:00:17,480 --> 01:00:20,960
Bring your attention back to the plan.

870
01:00:20,960 --> 01:00:24,580
Adjusting an outing, leaving early or
deciding against it can all be

871
01:00:24,580 --> 01:00:26,040
sensible choices.

872
01:00:26,040 --> 01:00:30,640
The useful question is whether the plan
fits your child and family.

873
01:00:30,640 --> 01:00:34,600
Keep the exit and recovery alongside the
practical details,

874
01:00:34,600 --> 01:00:38,680
and check the plan with your child in a
way they can access.

875
01:00:40,240 --> 01:00:43,440
Family life includes everyone's needs.

876
01:00:43,440 --> 01:00:44,860
Where there are siblings,

877
01:00:44,860 --> 01:00:48,800
small regular moments of individual
attention can matter.

878
01:00:48,800 --> 01:00:51,940
Fairness may mean each child getting what
they need,

879
01:00:51,940 --> 01:00:54,080
even when that looks different.

880
01:00:54,080 --> 01:00:57,680
Words that fit their age can help siblings
understand

881
01:00:57,680 --> 01:00:59,440
what is happening.

882
01:00:59,440 --> 01:01:02,380
Families are arranged in many different
ways.

883
01:01:02,380 --> 01:01:04,520
There isn't a formula for getting the
balance right,

884
01:01:04,520 --> 01:01:07,640
and you don't need to share private family
difficulties here.

885
01:01:07,640 --> 01:01:11,300
Look for something sustainable in your own
circumstances.

886
01:01:11,300 --> 01:01:14,280
The What works in our home page has space
for ideas

887
01:01:14,280 --> 01:01:15,800
if this topic is useful to you.

888
01:01:17,640 --> 01:01:21,480
Your own battery counts too. Helping
another person settle is harder

889
01:01:21,480 --> 01:01:22,040
when you are running

890
01:01:22,040 --> 01:01:27,910
on empty. On page thirteen, take a moment
with My own battery. What small

891
01:01:27,910 --> 01:01:30,040
thing helps you recharge?

892
01:01:30,040 --> 01:01:33,370
Who, if anyone, could help make this week
a little lighter? It might be a

893
01:01:33,370 --> 01:01:33,880
short walk, a

894
01:01:33,880 --> 01:01:37,830
few quiet minutes, a conversation or
practical help with a task. If

895
01:01:37,830 --> 01:01:39,000
support is scarce, that

896
01:01:39,000 --> 01:01:43,730
is a real constraint, not something you
have done wrong. Keep the idea

897
01:01:43,730 --> 01:01:44,480
realistic. Pause

898
01:01:44,480 --> 01:01:45,920
here to think or write.

899
01:01:54,400 --> 01:01:56,680
Keep whatever feels possible.

900
01:01:56,680 --> 01:01:59,880
Looking after your capacity is part of
family planning.

901
01:01:59,880 --> 01:02:02,760
Sometimes the useful step is reducing one
demand

902
01:02:02,760 --> 01:02:05,080
or asking for specific help.

903
01:02:05,080 --> 01:02:07,880
It says nothing about how much you love
your child,

904
01:02:07,880 --> 01:02:10,840
and it doesn't have to become another task
to succeed at.

905
01:02:13,200 --> 01:02:16,560
Families know a lot. Let's make room for
that knowledge.

906
01:02:16,560 --> 01:02:19,880
On page thirteen, choose one thing that
works in your home.

907
01:02:19,880 --> 01:02:23,840
It could relate to mornings, after school,
outings,

908
01:02:23,840 --> 01:02:26,840
bedtime, siblings or your own energy.

909
01:02:26,840 --> 01:02:29,600
At your table, each person can offer one
idea,

910
01:02:29,600 --> 01:02:31,680
with the option to pass.

911
01:02:31,680 --> 01:02:33,640
Listen for something that might fit your
family

912
01:02:33,640 --> 01:02:35,440
and write it down.

913
01:02:35,440 --> 01:02:39,080
We don't need to solve one family's whole
situation.

914
01:02:39,080 --> 01:02:42,280
If you're watching alone, note what
already works

915
01:02:42,280 --> 01:02:44,800
and one idea you might explore.

916
01:02:44,800 --> 01:02:46,280
Pause now for the sharing.

917
01:02:54,600 --> 01:02:58,840
Choose one idea to keep, and leave room to
adapt it.

918
01:02:58,840 --> 01:03:03,800
What works in another home may need to
look different in yours.

919
01:03:03,800 --> 01:03:07,480
As we move into partnership with school,
keep one observation from home

920
01:03:07,480 --> 01:03:08,600
in mind.

921
01:03:08,600 --> 01:03:11,640
It may be something school needs to
understand about your child's day.

922
01:03:18,240 --> 01:03:20,280
Now we turn to partnership with school.

923
01:03:20,280 --> 01:03:22,840
Keep that home observation in mind.

924
01:03:22,840 --> 01:03:24,440
Your child, your family and the school

925
01:03:24,440 --> 01:03:27,000
may each hold different parts of the
picture.

926
01:03:27,000 --> 01:03:30,800
Bringing those together can help shape
useful support.

927
01:03:30,800 --> 01:03:33,240
People arrive with very different school
experiences.

928
01:03:33,240 --> 01:03:34,800
Some relationships feel helpful,

929
01:03:34,800 --> 01:03:36,920
and others have been difficult.

930
01:03:36,920 --> 01:03:39,400
You can take part without sharing a
personal account.

931
01:03:39,400 --> 01:03:41,920
Please keep staff, schools and children

932
01:03:41,920 --> 01:03:44,100
unidentifiable in group discussion.

933
01:03:45,920 --> 01:03:50,050
Look at the overlapping partnerships:
family, school staff and support

934
01:03:50,050 --> 01:03:52,560
staff, with your child at the centre.

935
01:03:52,560 --> 01:03:56,920
Each person brings different knowledge and
a different relationship.

936
01:03:56,920 --> 01:04:01,200
Who else belongs in that picture for your
family: perhaps a grandparent,

937
01:04:01,200 --> 01:04:03,920
therapist or out-of-school-hours carer?

938
01:04:03,920 --> 01:04:06,920
What is the shared purpose of these
partnerships?

939
01:04:06,920 --> 01:04:10,920
How can your child's own communication
help shape it?

940
01:04:10,920 --> 01:04:15,920
Take a moment to reflect or discuss, and
pause if you'd like more time.

941
01:04:24,000 --> 01:04:27,200
Keep your child at the centre as you
consider the answer.

942
01:04:27,200 --> 01:04:30,000
No single person holds the whole picture.

943
01:04:30,000 --> 01:04:33,920
A useful partnership helps people
understand each other's knowledge

944
01:04:33,920 --> 01:04:36,880
and agree on support that makes a
difference to the child.

945
01:04:38,760 --> 01:04:42,390
The Family-School Partnerships Framework
recognises parents and

946
01:04:42,390 --> 01:04:45,760
families as children's first and
continuing educators.

947
01:04:45,760 --> 01:04:49,760
Learning happens across settings, and a
family's diversity and strengths

948
01:04:49,760 --> 01:04:51,320
matter to that learning.

949
01:04:51,320 --> 01:04:55,430
The other principles include community
involvement, mutual trust and

950
01:04:55,430 --> 01:04:56,760
shared responsibility,

951
01:04:56,760 --> 01:04:59,560
supported by committed and collaborative
leadership.

952
01:04:59,560 --> 01:05:04,130
In an everyday conversation, look for
respectful listening, clear

953
01:05:04,130 --> 01:05:08,200
information, your child's views and agreed
next steps.

954
01:05:09,760 --> 01:05:11,990
Family knowledge and school knowledge are
both needed from the

955
01:05:11,990 --> 01:05:12,760
beginning.

956
01:05:12,760 --> 01:05:15,900
Respectful listening is possible even when
people see a situation

957
01:05:15,900 --> 01:05:16,760
differently.

958
01:05:19,880 --> 01:05:22,200
What makes collaboration work?

959
01:05:22,200 --> 01:05:25,240
Think of a situation where people have
worked well together,

960
01:05:25,240 --> 01:05:28,240
inside or outside school.

961
01:05:28,240 --> 01:05:31,440
What helped them listen and make a
decision?

962
01:05:31,440 --> 01:05:34,720
Share a brief idea or reflect privately.

963
01:05:34,720 --> 01:05:36,640
Pause here if you would like time.

964
01:05:44,760 --> 01:05:49,750
The slide brings together shared goals and
values, mutual trust,

965
01:05:49,750 --> 01:05:51,680
consultation, curiosity

966
01:05:51,680 --> 01:05:55,120
and attention to different perspectives.

967
01:05:55,120 --> 01:05:57,560
Your child's voice belongs throughout.

968
01:05:57,560 --> 01:06:01,300
The phrase assume good intent may feel
difficult if trust has been

969
01:06:01,300 --> 01:06:02,280
damaged. Trust

970
01:06:02,280 --> 01:06:06,680
needs action from both sides, and it can
take time to rebuild.

971
01:06:06,680 --> 01:06:11,220
A possible opening is: how could we work
together so school goes well

972
01:06:11,220 --> 01:06:12,400
for my child?

973
01:06:12,400 --> 01:06:16,140
That gives the conversation a purpose
while leaving room to discuss

974
01:06:16,140 --> 01:06:17,360
what needs to change.

975
01:06:19,440 --> 01:06:24,070
Power can sit in the way a meeting is
arranged, the language used and

976
01:06:24,070 --> 01:06:25,120
who can make decisions.

977
01:06:25,120 --> 01:06:30,560
Familiarity with school systems, cultural
and historical experiences of

978
01:06:30,560 --> 01:06:31,600
education,

979
01:06:31,600 --> 01:06:35,280
language and financial circumstances can
also shape how a conversation

980
01:06:35,280 --> 01:06:35,680
feels.

981
01:06:35,680 --> 01:06:41,120
Who sets the agenda? Whose knowledge gets
heard? Is your child included

982
01:06:41,120 --> 01:06:42,400
in a way that works for them?

983
01:06:42,400 --> 01:06:45,440
What might need to change about the
process?

984
01:06:46,560 --> 01:06:49,760
Naming these features can help us plan a
more balanced conversation.

985
01:06:49,760 --> 01:06:53,760
We can examine the meeting itself without
turning the discussion into a

986
01:06:53,760 --> 01:06:56,400
judgement about an identifiable person.

987
01:06:58,760 --> 01:07:03,800
Use the Power dynamics activity on page
fifteen, or three sticky notes.

988
01:07:03,800 --> 01:07:07,280
First, describe an imbalance from a home
perspective.

989
01:07:07,280 --> 01:07:10,200
On the second, consider one from a school
perspective.

990
01:07:10,200 --> 01:07:14,600
On the third, suggest how the imbalance
could be addressed.

991
01:07:14,600 --> 01:07:17,840
Thinking about another perspective doesn't
mean excusing poor practice.

992
01:07:17,840 --> 01:07:21,800
It helps us identify what could change in
a conversation.

993
01:07:21,800 --> 01:07:26,120
You can share at your table, add anonymous
notes under Home, School

994
01:07:26,120 --> 01:07:29,400
and Solutions, or keep your notes private.

995
01:07:29,400 --> 01:07:30,680
Pause now for the activity.

996
01:07:38,720 --> 01:07:42,040
Look again at the solutions. Could the
agenda be shared earlier?

997
01:07:42,040 --> 01:07:43,880
Could language be clearer,

998
01:07:43,880 --> 01:07:46,000
a support person be included,

999
01:07:46,000 --> 01:07:47,560
or another way of contributing

1000
01:07:47,560 --> 01:07:49,080
be available?

1001
01:07:49,080 --> 01:07:52,760
Choose one idea that would make
participation more workable.

1002
01:07:52,760 --> 01:07:55,000
Keep it for the conversation plan later in
